WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.223
In part one

00:00:01.223 --> 00:00:03.760
the array queue ran out of room

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:06.220
front and rear only move right.

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:07.527
Here is the fix.

00:00:07.527 --> 00:00:09.680
Bend the cells into a ring

00:00:09.757 --> 00:00:12.140
so that the cell after the last

00:00:12.217 --> 00:00:13.832
one is the first one.

00:00:13.832 --> 00:00:15.139
After cell four

00:00:15.139 --> 00:00:17.753
the next cell is not off the end

00:00:17.753 --> 00:00:19.060
it is cell zero.

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:23.106
To step from index i to the next cell

00:00:23.106 --> 00:00:26.802
compute (i + 1) mod capacity.

00:00:26.802 --> 00:00:31.179
The indices run zero, one, two, three, four

00:00:31.179 --> 00:00:32.930
and back to zero.

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:36.571
In Java, % is the remainder operator

00:00:36.571 --> 00:00:39.251
with capacity five,

00:00:39.251 --> 00:00:41.011
(4 + 1) % 5 is 0.

00:00:41.088 --> 00:00:43.231
This is called a ring buffer

00:00:43.307 --> 00:00:44.762
or circular buffer.

00:00:44.762 --> 00:00:47.824
Nothing in memory is actually circular

00:00:47.824 --> 00:00:50.120
only the index arithmetic is.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:52.765
The cleanest circular queue

00:00:52.842 --> 00:00:54.831
stores two numbers: front

00:00:54.831 --> 00:00:58.043
the index of the front element, and size

00:00:58.043 --> 00:00:59.879
the number of elements.

00:00:59.879 --> 00:01:02.862
Here front is three and size is three

00:01:02.862 --> 00:01:06.075
so the elements sit in cells three, four

00:01:06.075 --> 00:01:08.140
and, wrapping round, zero.

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:10.261
The rear is computed

00:01:10.261 --> 00:01:14.057
(front + size - 1) mod capacity.

00:01:14.057 --> 00:01:17.093
Three plus three minus one is five

00:01:17.093 --> 00:01:19.370
and five mod five is zero.

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:22.692
Enqueue puts the new element in the

00:01:22.777 --> 00:01:25.409
first free cell after the rear

00:01:25.409 --> 00:01:29.231
(front + size) mod capacity, here cell one

00:01:29.231 --> 00:01:30.929
and increases size.

00:01:30.929 --> 00:01:33.986
Dequeue takes the element at front

00:01:33.986 --> 00:01:36.533
moves front one step forward

00:01:36.533 --> 00:01:39.760
mod capacity, and decreases size.

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:42.159
Why keep size at all

00:01:42.159 --> 00:01:45.017
rather than just front and rear?

00:01:45.017 --> 00:01:46.317
Fill the ring

00:01:46.317 --> 00:01:49.088
front is three and rear is two.

00:01:49.088 --> 00:01:52.380
The cell after the rear is the front.

00:01:52.730 --> 00:01:55.557
Now empty it by dequeuing everything

00:01:55.557 --> 00:01:58.231
front has moved on to three again

00:01:58.231 --> 00:01:59.988
and rear is still two.

00:01:59.988 --> 00:02:03.425
Again, the cell after the rear is the front.

00:02:03.425 --> 00:02:05.488
With only the two indices

00:02:05.565 --> 00:02:07.933
a full queue and an empty queue

00:02:08.009 --> 00:02:09.690
look exactly the same.

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:12.708
So every circular queue needs one

00:02:12.789 --> 00:02:14.891
more piece of information.

00:02:14.891 --> 00:02:17.397
Keep a size counter, as we do

00:02:17.478 --> 00:02:20.307
or use special values such as

00:02:20.388 --> 00:02:22.248
−1 for an empty queue

00:02:22.248 --> 00:02:24.592
or never fill the last cell

00:02:24.673 --> 00:02:27.260
so a queue with capacity n holds

00:02:27.341 --> 00:02:29.685
at most n minus one elements.

00:02:29.685 --> 00:02:33.000
That is the choice in the CLRS textbook.

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:35.101
Here is the class

00:02:35.101 --> 00:02:38.651
with capacity four for the slides' trace.

00:02:38.651 --> 00:02:42.285
Enqueue first throws if the queue is full

00:02:42.285 --> 00:02:44.820
size equals the array length.

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:48.720
Then int rear equals front plus size

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.215
% array.length

00:02:51.215 --> 00:02:54.669
the first free cell after the rear.

00:02:54.669 --> 00:02:58.316
It stores x there and increases size.

00:02:58.316 --> 00:03:01.770
Enqueue 14 goes to cell zero.

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:05.348
Enqueue 22 goes to cell one

00:03:05.348 --> 00:03:07.680
and 13 to cell two.

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:11.128
Dequeue throws on an empty queue.

00:03:11.128 --> 00:03:14.951
Otherwise it takes x equals array[front]

00:03:14.951 --> 00:03:15.820
14.

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:18.551
Front moves one step forward

00:03:18.551 --> 00:03:20.521
mod the length, to one

00:03:20.521 --> 00:03:22.409
and size drops to two.

00:03:22.409 --> 00:03:24.133
It returns 14.

00:03:24.133 --> 00:03:27.170
The rear, front plus size minus one

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:28.730
is still cell two.

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:31.674
The demo prints each step.

00:03:31.674 --> 00:03:34.946
Enqueue 15 goes to cell three

00:03:34.946 --> 00:03:36.310
the last cell.

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:39.939
Enqueue 4: the last cell is taken

00:03:39.939 --> 00:03:43.926
but cell zero is free, and 4 lands there.

00:03:43.926 --> 00:03:46.496
Check it by hand: before it

00:03:46.496 --> 00:03:49.154
front was one and size three

00:03:49.154 --> 00:03:52.965
so the new element goes to one plus three

00:03:52.965 --> 00:03:55.180
mod four, which is zero.

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:57.181
Read from the front

00:03:57.181 --> 00:04:00.813
the queue is 22, 13, 15,

00:04:00.813 --> 00:04:01.308
4.

00:04:01.308 --> 00:04:03.371
The rear, at index zero

00:04:03.371 --> 00:04:06.920
is to the left of the front, at index one.

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:09.232
That is what wrapped means.

00:04:09.232 --> 00:04:11.130
And the queue is full.

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:15.072
Continue the trace: dequeue twice

00:04:15.072 --> 00:04:16.950
then enqueue 9.

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:19.700
Which cells hold which values

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:22.500
and what are front, size and rear?

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:25.140
Pause the video and work it out.

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:31.748
The two dequeues return 22

00:04:31.837 --> 00:04:32.994
and 13.

00:04:32.994 --> 00:04:36.467
Front moves to 3, and size is 2

00:04:36.467 --> 00:04:38.960
the queue is 15, 4.

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:42.524
Enqueue 9 goes to 3 + 2

00:04:42.524 --> 00:04:45.113
mod 4, which is cell 1.

00:04:45.113 --> 00:04:48.862
The cells are 4, 9, empty, 15

00:04:48.862 --> 00:04:52.790
with front 3, size 3, and rear 1.

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:54.148
Two traps.

00:04:54.148 --> 00:04:56.949
If you forget the % capacity

00:04:57.027 --> 00:04:58.972
front + size runs past

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:00.684
the end of the array

00:05:00.762 --> 00:05:03.096
and Java throws an array index

00:05:03.174 --> 00:05:05.042
out of bounds exception.

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:07.143
The array is only circular

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:09.010
because of the modulus.

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:12.949
Second, when you step an index backward

00:05:12.949 --> 00:05:15.821
as the Deque of part three does

00:05:15.821 --> 00:05:19.858
-1 % 5 is -1 in Java

00:05:19.858 --> 00:05:20.756
not 4.

00:05:20.756 --> 00:05:23.986
So write i - 1 + capacity

00:05:23.986 --> 00:05:25.960
all % capacity.

00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:28.940
The slides also give a version

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:32.220
that stores front and rear, and no size.

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:35.740
Both start at -1, which means empty.

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:38.835
Enqueue first checks for overflow

00:05:38.835 --> 00:05:41.903
the queue is full when rear + 1

00:05:41.903 --> 00:05:43.760
mod MAX, equals front.

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:45.617
If the queue is empty

00:05:45.698 --> 00:05:47.959
the first element sets front

00:05:48.039 --> 00:05:49.412
and rear to 0

00:05:49.412 --> 00:05:52.561
otherwise rear moves forward, mod MAX.

00:05:52.561 --> 00:05:55.548
With MAX 5, enqueue 10 to 50

00:05:55.548 --> 00:05:57.970
rear walks from 0 to 4.

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:00.686
Dequeue takes the front element.

00:06:00.686 --> 00:06:02.904
If that was the only element

00:06:02.904 --> 00:06:04.309
front equals rear

00:06:04.309 --> 00:06:06.748
and both are reset to -1

00:06:06.748 --> 00:06:09.040
otherwise front moves forward.

00:06:09.620 --> 00:06:13.247
Dequeue gives 10, and front moves to 1.

00:06:13.247 --> 00:06:15.147
Dequeue gives 20

00:06:15.147 --> 00:06:17.220
and front moves to 2.

00:06:17.570 --> 00:06:21.618
Enqueue 60: rear wraps from 4 to 0.

00:06:21.618 --> 00:06:24.930
Enqueue 70: rear moves to 1.

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:28.611
Now rear + 1 is 2, which is front

00:06:28.611 --> 00:06:32.023
the queue is full, and 80 is refused

00:06:32.104 --> 00:06:34.135
even though nothing is at

00:06:34.216 --> 00:06:35.922
the end of the array.

00:06:36.003 --> 00:06:38.359
We added that last enqueue to

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:40.633
see the overflow test fire.

00:06:40.633 --> 00:06:41.446
And note

00:06:41.446 --> 00:06:44.858
one element looks like front equals rear

00:06:44.858 --> 00:06:46.401
both real indices

00:06:46.401 --> 00:06:48.919
while empty is both minus one.

00:06:48.919 --> 00:06:51.600
That is why dequeue resets them.

00:06:52.180 --> 00:06:54.309
Circular queues are everywhere

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:55.515
in systems code.

00:06:55.586 --> 00:06:57.644
A ring buffer reuses the same

00:06:57.715 --> 00:07:00.696
block of memory forever: keyboard buffers

00:07:00.696 --> 00:07:02.612
audio and network buffers

00:07:02.612 --> 00:07:05.095
and logging systems work this way.

00:07:05.095 --> 00:07:08.147
And a queue can cycle through a fixed set.

00:07:08.147 --> 00:07:10.702
The traffic light queue holds zero

00:07:10.702 --> 00:07:13.540
one and two, for red, yellow and green.

00:07:13.890 --> 00:07:17.605
Every minute, dequeue the light whose turn

00:07:17.693 --> 00:07:20.877
it is and print it: minute one, red.

00:07:20.877 --> 00:07:22.912
Then enqueue it again

00:07:22.912 --> 00:07:25.477
back at the end of the line.

00:07:25.477 --> 00:07:28.130
Yellow, green, and red again.

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:31.019
Operating systems use the same trick

00:07:31.019 --> 00:07:33.146
called round-robin scheduling

00:07:33.215 --> 00:07:35.136
to give each waiting process

00:07:35.205 --> 00:07:36.440
a turn on the CPU.

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:40.309
Here are the four queues side by side.

00:07:40.309 --> 00:07:43.771
Every one of them enqueues in O(1)

00:07:43.857 --> 00:07:45.934
but the linear array may

00:07:46.021 --> 00:07:48.011
refuse with free cells.

00:07:48.011 --> 00:07:51.300
Dequeue is O(n) only when we shift.

00:07:51.650 --> 00:07:53.894
The circular array is usually the

00:07:53.962 --> 00:07:55.729
fastest queue in practice

00:07:55.729 --> 00:07:58.041
no node is allocated per element

00:07:58.109 --> 00:07:59.808
and the cells sit next to

00:07:59.876 --> 00:08:01.304
each other in memory.

00:08:01.304 --> 00:08:03.547
Its limit is the fixed capacity.

00:08:03.615 --> 00:08:05.179
Doubling the array when

00:08:05.247 --> 00:08:06.743
it is full removes it

00:08:06.743 --> 00:08:09.530
and that is what Java's ArrayDeque does.

00:08:10.110 --> 00:08:11.119
To sum up.

00:08:11.119 --> 00:08:13.741
One: in a circular queue

00:08:13.741 --> 00:08:16.666
the next cell is

00:08:16.666 --> 00:08:18.481
(i + 1) mod the capacity

00:08:18.481 --> 00:08:21.910
so the array behaves like a ring.

00:08:22.260 --> 00:08:25.181
Two: front and rear alone cannot

00:08:25.272 --> 00:08:27.188
tell full from empty.

00:08:27.188 --> 00:08:29.105
Keep a size counter

00:08:29.105 --> 00:08:31.660
or use minus one for empty.

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:35.160
Three: every operation is O(1)

00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:37.410
within a fixed capacity.

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:38.712
Try this.

00:08:38.712 --> 00:08:42.413
A circular queue of capacity 5.

00:08:42.413 --> 00:08:45.691
Enqueue 1, 2, 3, 4

00:08:45.691 --> 00:08:49.921
dequeue 3 times; then enqueue 5,

00:08:49.921 --> 00:08:51.507
6 and 7.

00:08:51.507 --> 00:08:56.160
Write down the cells, front, size and rear.

00:08:56.510 --> 00:08:57.544
In part 3

00:08:57.544 --> 00:08:59.907
queues that do not follow FIFO.

00:08:59.907 --> 00:09:03.230
Priority queues, and the double-ended queue.

